      },
      "message": "Potential HTTP verb confusion. `HEAD` is routed like `GET` but `request.get?` will return `false`",
      "note": "Inverted: here request.get? being false DENIES rather than admits. demo_may_look? returns false for HEAD, and every caller treats false as refusal \u2014 require_write_access falls through to deny_access, ensure_full_access to the access_denied redirect. Verified 2026-09-03 by request: GET a demo-readable page returns 200, HEAD the same page returns 302 to the login. HEAD is more restricted than GET, which is the opposite of the bypass this check warns about.",
      "render_path": null,
      "user_input": null,
      "warning_code": 121,
      "warning_type": "HTTP Verb Confusion"
    },
    {
      "fingerprint": "1b9270c3a7a6e0b5fea373cf2aa435d10163b2327be0ce9926cbb04395042735",
      "note": "Sanitised, and sudo-only at the source. A custom language's help text is written only through LanguagesController, which is require_sudo_only; Language#rendered_doc then runs the RedCloth output through Rails::Html::SafeListSanitizer before it reaches this template. No non-sudo input can reach it, and what sudo writes is stripped to the safe list like any other CMS body."
    },
    {
      "fingerprint": "64b6846c2ab763c079fea3a5e86f873c9ddfcd8d037e9cc31f5565ed848607c9",
      "note": "Harmless direction. request.get? being false on a HEAD request only means the return-to URL is not remembered, so the coadmin lands on the claim page and then goes to the default instead of back where they were. A HEAD request is never a person navigating to a page they want to return to. The redirect to claim_path is unconditional and is what actually gates the draft."
    }
